    overview: "Denmark is globally famous for hygge (cosiness and wellbeing), consistently ranks as one of the world's happiest countries, and has one of Europe's most robust social welfare systems. Copenhagen is a world design capital and a major tech hub (home to The Lego Group, Maersk, and a thriving startup scene). Denmark's combination of high salaries, excellent public services, and work-life culture makes it extremely attractive for skilled workers.",
    costOfLiving: "Denmark is expensive. Copenhagen living costs average DKK 20,000–32,000/month ($2,900–$4,600). Rent is the biggest expense — a 1-bedroom city centre apartment costs DKK 10,000–17,000/month ($1,450–$2,460). Cycling everywhere saves substantial transport costs. Food and alcohol are expensive. Outside Copenhagen, Aarhus and Odense are 20–25% more affordable.",
    visaOverview: "EU citizens have freedom of movement. Non-EU nationals need a Pay Limit Scheme Permit, a Positive List Permit (for in-demand occupations), a Fast Track Scheme Permit (via certified companies), or an Entrepreneur Permit. The Researcher/Lecturer Permit covers academic positions. Permanent residency after 4 years; citizenship after 9 years (6 for spouses).",
    whyMoveHere: "Denmark delivers the full hygge experience — excellent work-life balance, world-class cycling infrastructure, progressive values, outstanding healthcare and education, and a deeply democratic, egalitarian social fabric. Copenhagen's food scene (multiple Michelin 3-star restaurants including Noma's legacy) is world-famous. Danish design permeates everyday life.",
    topCities: ["Copenhagen", "Aarhus", "Odense", "Aalborg", "Esbjerg"],
    practicalTips: ["Cycling is not optional in Copenhagen — buy a quality bike on day one", "CPR number (civil registration number) is needed for everything — register immediately", "Danish weather is grey and windy much of the year — Scandinavian attitude to outdoor life helps", "Janteloven (don't think you're better than anyone) is a real cultural dynamic", "Danish language is genuinely difficult — English works everywhere, but learning Danish matters for long-term integration"]
