    overview: "Cambodia is Southeast Asia's most surprisingly liveable budget destination — a country of ancient Khmer temples (Angkor Wat is the world's largest religious monument), the mighty Mekong River, and a rapidly developing economy with a large and growing expat community. Phnom Penh is an increasingly sophisticated capital; Siem Reap offers proximity to the temple complex; Kampot and Koh Rong offer relaxed coastal living.",
    costOfLiving: "Cambodia is one of Asia's cheapest countries for expat living. The USD is Cambodia's de facto currency (alongside the Riel). A comfortable lifestyle in Phnom Penh or Siem Reap costs $600–$1,200/month. A 1-bedroom apartment in a nice central area costs $300–$600/month. A full restaurant meal costs $3–$8. Cambodia offers extraordinary value for those earning in hard currencies.",
    visaOverview: "Cambodia offers 30-day tourist visas on arrival or e-visa for most nationalities. The 'Business Visa' (EB) can be renewed indefinitely with a simple monthly renewal process and is widely used by long-term expats and digital nomads. The Ordinary Resident Visa (ER) exists for those with Cambodian employers or property ownership. Cambodia has no formal digital nomad visa.",
    whyMoveHere: "Cambodia offers ultra-low cost living, warm year-round climate, Angkor Wat on your doorstep, surprisingly good food (amok, lok lak, fresh Kampot pepper), a welcoming local population, and an expat community that punches above its weight in quality. The country's rapid development creates genuine business opportunities for enterprising expats.",
