    overview: "Uruguay is South America's most progressive and stable small country — a democracy of 3.5 million people wedged between Brazil and Argentina. Montevideo is a relaxed, sophisticated capital with excellent quality of life, and Punta del Este is one of South America's most glamorous beach resorts. Uruguay has been the first country to legalise marijuana, has strong social welfare, and attracts a growing number of remote workers and retirees seeking South American stability.",
    costOfLiving: "Uruguay is one of South America's most expensive countries, though still affordable by global standards. Montevideo living costs run $1,200–$2,200/month for a comfortable lifestyle. Punta del Este is significantly more expensive in summer season. Uruguay has a strong social safety net funded by relatively high taxes. Property values are stable and legally transparent — unusual for the region.",
    visaOverview: "Most nationalities get 90-day tourist visa-free entry. Uruguay offers a clear permanent residency path after 3–5 years for those who can demonstrate income, employment, or family ties. The Rentista Visa requires $1,500+/month income. Uruguay does not have a specific digital nomad visa but the immigration system is relatively straightforward and legally transparent.",
    whyMoveHere: "Uruguay offers the region's most stable political system, genuine rule of law (rare in South America), low corruption, strong democratic institutions, good English-language schools, and a relaxed River Plate culture (shared with neighbouring Argentina). Colonia del Sacramento, just across from Buenos Aires, is a UNESCO World Heritage colonial town of great charm.",
